   138	## 2. Why the four printed corners are forced
   139	
   140	Put `t=x` after `X=t^-1`.  In Moh's “suitable variables,” after the implicit
   141	linear normalization that absorbs the minor top term `a_{-1}t^-1`, evaluation
   142	on the radius-two probe is the exact identity
   143	
   144	```text
   145	Omega(P)(t,(pi-a_2)/t)
   146	  = P(t^-1,a_0+a_1*t+pi*t^2).
   147	```
   148	
   149	Thus a new-coordinate monomial `x^e y^k` has probe weight `e-k`.  P.209 places
   150	this probe in the selected minor disc `D_2^*`, so its center lies strictly over
   151	`delta_3=-1`.  Since `g_sigma` is nonconstant, the same truncation is also a
   152	pi-root of the product `g*T_1^psi*T_2^psi`.  These are the center/product
   153	hypotheses of Proposition 6.1 (pp.191--193); `ord g(sigma)=-18<0` then makes
   154	the probe a distribution detector for `g,T_1^psi=f,T_2^psi`.  Their degrees have gcd 11,
   155	and their local degrees are `27,18,15`, so Definition 3.1 gives, for a monic
   156	cubic `H`,
   157	
   158	```text
   159	g(sigma)   ~ H(pi)^9 t^-18,
   160	f(sigma)   ~ H(pi)^6 t^-12,
   161	T_2(sigma) ~ H(pi)^5 t^-10.
   162	```
   163	
   164	Here `~` retains the relevant nonzero leading scalar; compatible
   165	leading-coefficient normalizations may make the displayed faces monic.
