   230	## 3. Shape from old-coordinate polynomiality
   231	
   232	Let `P(X,Y)=sum_{i+j<=D} p_ij X^iY^j`.  Its literal transform is
   233	
   234	```text
   235	Omega(P)=sum p_ij x^-i(a_0+a_1x+a_2x^2+x^3y)^j.                 (3.1)
   236	```
   237	
   238	Conversely, on a monomial,
   239	
   240	```text
   241	Omega^-1(x^e y^k)
   242	 = X^(k-e) [X^2(Y-a_0)-a_1X-a_2]^k.                            (3.2)
   243	```
   244	
   245	Equations (3.1)--(3.2), not a guessed polygon, are the exact polynomiality
   246	test: negative `X` powers and terms of total degree greater than `D` must cancel
   247	after coefficients with the same image are combined.  A single old monomial
   248	can only produce
   249	
   250	```text
   251	0<=k<=D,             4k-D <= e <= 2D+k,
   252	```
   253	
   254	but membership in this envelope is not sufficient, because inverse-image
   255	coefficients are correlated.
