   101	This corrects the literal “squarefreeness/coprimality of \(p,q\)” formulation
   102	in the lane question. The inequation belongs to \(R,H\), while \(q\) itself is
   103	squarefree.
   104	
   105	### 2.1 The non-bottom ODE and the bottom exception
   106	
   107	**SOURCE-READ.** After the common power is stripped as in Appendix-I
   108	Propositions A.3--A.4, every node to which that argument applies satisfies
   109	
   110	\[
   111	  D(P_j,Q_j,p_j,q_j)=P_jp_jq_j'-Q_jq_jp_j'=c_jp_j,
   112	  \qquad c_j\in k^\times.                                      \tag{2.1}
   113	\]
   114	
   115	Here a prime is differentiation with respect to the displayed polynomial
   116	variable \(\pi\). Elsewhere in the paper decorations such as \(q^*\) are
   117	labels; they must not silently be interpreted as derivatives.
   118	
   119	At \(r=1\), Proposition 4.6 and Proposition A.5 give a different equation.
   120	After dividing the common gcd, it has the form
   121	
   122	\[
   123	  d\,g(\pi)f'(\pi)-e\,f(\pi)g'(\pi)=\kappa,
   124	  \qquad \kappa\in k^\times,                                  \tag{2.2}
   125	\]
   126	
   127	up to the harmless convention that interchanges \((d,e;g,f)\) and changes
   128	the sign. Equation (2.2) forces \(g\) and \(f\) to be squarefree and coprime:
   129	a repeated root or a common root would make its left side vanish there. It is
   130	not legitimate to replace its right side by \(cp\).
   131	
   132	Nor is it legitimate to put (2.1) or (2.2) on every factor called a “minor
   133	sibling.” Proposition 5.3 constructs the next node from a factor in the major
   134	window. The minor-disc discussion on page 200 supplies distribution/radius
   135	bounds, not a specified next general point with a printed child ODE. A minor
   136	factor remains represented in the parent multiplicity passport; attaching a
   137	new bottom equation to it is an extra hypothesis.
