   139	### 2.2 What page 201's Galois action does and does not say
   140	
   141	**SOURCE-READ.** The displayed automorphism on page 201 is
   142	\(\bar t\mapsto\omega\bar t\) over
   143	\(k\langle\langle\bar t^{A_j}\rangle\rangle\), for an
   144	\(A_j\)-th root of unity \(\omega\). The page does not literally print
   145	“\(\pi\mapsto\zeta\pi\)” for both \(p_j\) and \(q_j\).
   146	
   147	**PROVED-HERE, conditional chart form.** Once the chosen chart is shown to
   148	carry this action diagonally on its coefficient coordinate, it is safe to use
   149	the semi-invariant ansatz
   150	
   151	\[
   152	  p(\pi)=\lambda_p\pi^bF(\pi^A),\qquad
   153	  q(\pi)=\lambda_q\pi^eG(\pi^A),                               \tag{2.3}
   154	\]
   155	
   156	where \(b\ge0\) is the fixed-zero multiplicity, \(e\in\{0,1\}\), and
   157	\(P\equiv b\pmod A,\ Q\equiv e\pmod A\). The semi-invariant character is
   158	\(b\bmod A\). Nonzero roots occur in full \(A\)-orbits; if \(b>0\),
   159	root containment forces \(e=1\). In this report
   160	(2.3) is a **DERIVED-ANSATZ**, not attributed verbatim to page 201.
   161	
   162	Writing \(z=\pi^A\), direct differentiation gives the exact quotient formula
   163	
   164	\[
   165	\begin{split}
   166	 D(P,Q,p,q)=\lambda_p\lambda_q\pi^{b+e-1}{&
   167	 (Pe-Qb)F(z)G(z)\\
   168	 &+Az[P F(z)G'(z)-QG(z)F'(z)]\} .                 \tag{2.4}
   169	\end{split}
   170	\]
   171	
   172	This identity is the coefficient engine used in every computation below.
