   174	## 3. The exact recentering calculation: determined versus missing data
   175	
   176	Let \(C\) be a root of \(p_j\) of multiplicity
   177	\(u>d_j/(n-M_j)\), and put
   178	
   179	\[
   180	  \epsilon=\delta_{j-1}-\delta_j>0,\qquad
   181	  X=C+Yt^\epsilon.
   182	\]
   183	
   184	Then
   185	
   186	\[
   187	 \sigma_{j-1}(Y)=
   188	 \sum_{\nu<\delta_j}a_\nu t^\nu+C t^{\delta_j}
   189	       +\sum_{\delta_j<\nu<\delta_{j-1}}b_\nu t^\nu
   190	       +Yt^{\delta_{j-1}}.                                    \tag{3.1}
   191	\]
   192	
   193	This is the precise meaning of “the parent factor recentered and rescaled.”
   194	Proposition 5.3 determines the child degrees
   195	
   196	\[
   197	 P_{j-1}=u{d_{j-1}\over d_j},\qquad
   198	 Q_{j-1}=u{n-M_{j-1}\over d_j},                               \tag{3.2}
   199	\]
   200	
   201	and its displayed product formula determines \(\delta_{j-1}\). In the lane's
   202	index convention it is
   203	
   204	\[
   205	 \delta_{r-1}=1-
   206	 { (n-M_{r-1})\prod_{i=r}^{s}[V_i(n-M_i)-d_i]
   207	  \over
   208	   (n-M_s-1)\prod_{i=r}^{s}[V_i(n-M_{i-1})-d_i]} .              \tag{3.3}
   209	\]
   210	
   211	The same proposition fixes the old coefficients below \(\delta_j\) and the
   212	new coefficient \(C\) at \(\delta_j\). It does not say that the coefficients
   213	of the child's stripped \(p,q\) are obtained by Taylor-expanding the parent's
   214	stripped \(p,q\).
   215	
   216	### 3.1 Jet lemma
   217	
   218	The exact reason can be stated without any conjectural geometry. For any raw
   219	polynomial or approximate root \(K\), write its whole expansion at the parent
   220	chart as
   221	
   222	\[
   223	 K(\sigma_j(X))=t^\lambda\sum_{\rho\ge0}K_\rho(X)t^\rho .      \tag{3.4}
   224	\]
   225	
   226	Put
   227	\[
   228	 B(t)=\sum_{\delta_j<\nu<\delta_{j-1}}b_\nu t^{\nu-\delta_j}.
   229	\]
   230	Suppose \(K_0\) has multiplicity \(w\) at \(C\), and the characteristic
   231	conditions make \(w\epsilon\) the child's first weight. Substitution of
   232	\(X=C+B(t)+Yt^\epsilon\) into (3.4) gives
   233	
   234	\[
   235	 \operatorname{in}_{j-1}K(Y)=
   236	 [t^{w\epsilon}]
   237	 \sum_{\rho,k\ge0}{K_\rho^{(k)}(C)\over k!}
   238	       (B(t)+Yt^\epsilon)^k t^\rho.                            \tag{3.5}
   239	\]
   240	
   241	For \(B=0\), this reduces to
   242	\(\sum_{k=0}^wK_{(w-k)\epsilon}^{(k)}(C)Y^k/k!\), with absent
   243	exponents contributing zero. In general the new \(b_\nu\)'s enter as well.
   244	Since \(B\) has strictly positive order, the coefficient of \(Y^w\) is still
   245	\(K_0^{(w)}(C)/w!\). Hence:
   246	
   247	| Datum | Determined by parent \(p_j,q_j\)? |
   248	|---|---|
   249	| inherited series coefficients below \(\delta_j\) | yes, as chart data, not as coefficients of \(p_{j-1},q_{j-1}\) |
   250	| centre \(C\) and factor multiplicity \(u\) | yes |
   251	| child radius and degrees | yes, by Prop. 5.3 |
   252	| coefficient of \(Y^w\) in a raw child initial form | yes: \(K_0^{(w)}(C)/w!\) |
   253	| coefficients of \(Y^0,\ldots,Y^{w-1}\) | no: they involve new \(b_\nu\)'s and \(K_\rho\) for \(\rho>0\) |
   254	| child \(q_{j-1}\) from parent \(q_j\) | no direct map: they come from \(T_{j-1}\) and \(T_j\), respectively |
   255	| siblings in one cyclic orbit | one prototype plus Galois transforms, once the chart action is established |
   256	
   257	The “no” entries are free gluing variables before the child ODE and shared-jet
   258	equations; they are not unconstrained coefficients.
   259	
   260	Equation (3.5) is **PROVED-HERE** by Taylor expansion. It identifies exactly
   261	the missing variables: higher weighted jets of the *same global* \(f,g\) and
   262	of their approximate roots.
   263	
   264	If
   265	
   266	\[
   267	 h_C=\left.{p_j(X)\over(X-C)^u}\right|_{X=C}\ne0,              \tag{3.6}
   268	\]
   269	
   270	then the top raw coefficient acquired on recentering \(g\) is multiplied by
   271	\(h_C^{n/d_j}\). **PROVED-HERE, not quoted from Moh:** after retaining the
   272	same raw outer units and fixing the root-of-unity gauge,
   273	the next non-bottom leading coefficients can be chosen as
   274	
   275	\[
   276	 \operatorname{lc}(p_{j-1})=h_C^{d_{j-1}/d_j},\qquad
   277	 \operatorname{lc}(q_{j-1})=h_C^{(n-M_{j-1})/d_j}.             \tag{3.7}
   278	\]
   279	
   280	At a bottom child these become the raw leading-unit relations
   281	
   282	\[
   283	 \operatorname{lc}(g_{\rm child})=h_C^{n/d_2},\qquad
   284	 \operatorname{lc}(f_{\rm child})=h_C^{m/d_2},                \tag{3.8}
   285	\]
   286	
   287	after a common outer-unit normalization. These relations are normalization
   288	dependent: if the stripped child polynomials are made monic, the same powers
   289	move into their raw constants. They therefore impose finite power/root choices,
   290	but do not identify any of the lower monic coefficients.
   291	
   292	For a nonzero orbit root \(r^A=\alpha_i\) of
   293	\(p=\lambda_p\pi^b\prod_k(\pi^A-\alpha_k)^{u_k}\), formula (3.6) is explicitly
   294	
   295	\[
   296	 h_r=\lambda_p r^b(Ar^{A-1})^{u_i}
   297	        \prod_{k\ne i}(\alpha_i-\alpha_k)^{u_k}.                \tag{3.9}
   298	\]
   299	
   300	For the zero factor it is
   301	\(h_0=\lambda_p\prod_k(-\alpha_k)^{u_k}\). These are the only
   302	coefficient-level parent-to-child formulas obtainable from the leading
   303	polynomial alone.
   304	
   305	### 3.2 Consequence for the requested “exact two-node system”
   306	
   307	**OPEN[SOURCE-JET-GLUING].** There is no exact functor
   308	
   309	\[
   310	 (p_j,q_j,C,u)\longmapsto(p_{j-1},q_{j-1})
   311	\]
   312	
   313	in the cited pages. Any system claiming otherwise has silently set, identified,
   314	or existentially forgotten the positive-\(\rho\) coefficients in (3.5). The
   315	source-exact full system must instead introduce a bounded jet of the common
   316	global \(f,g\), impose both substitutions (3.1), construct both approximate
   317	roots, and then extract the two initial forms. Those jet variables and their
   318	support were not part of the frozen tree rows.
   319	
   320	This is also why merely requiring “one coefficient field” adds no equation.
   321	Finitely many algebraic local solutions over \(\overline{\mathbf Q}\) live in
   322	their compositum; parametrized solutions live over a common finitely generated
   323	field. What can obstruct them is equality of their underlying global jet
   324	coefficients, not the field containing those coefficients.
